WEBVTT

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:05.311
So, yeah, we've been asked a lot over the years.
所以，是的，这些年来我们经常被问到。

00:00:05.311 --> 00:00:06.395
What was the work?
那个词是什么？

00:00:06.395 --> 00:00:07.419
What did Ted say?
泰德说了什么？

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:09.989
It's created a lot of debate over the years.
这些年来这引发了大量的争论。

00:00:09.989 --> 00:00:10.806
You know what?
你知道吗？

00:00:10.806 --> 00:00:15.420
After all this time, 19 years later, to hell with it, I'm just going to say it.
时隔这么久，19年过去了，管他呢，我今天就要说出来。

00:00:15.419 --> 00:00:18.607
I'm just going to officially say what the word was.
我要正式说出那个词到底是什么。

00:00:18.607 --> 00:00:20.420
The word that Ted said was...
泰德说的那个词是……

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:41.320
the show really helped me get used to the sound of the language, learn new vocabulary, and practice grammar.
这部剧真的帮我习惯了这门语言的语音，学习新的词汇，并练习语法。

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:49.776
I remember I used to watch the same episodes several times, and it actually helped me pass my exams at university.
我记得我以前会把同一集看好几遍，这实际上确实帮我通过了大学考试。

00:00:49.776 --> 00:00:54.597
And for me, it's a show that I discovered completely by accident.
对我来说，这是一部纯属偶然发现的剧。

00:00:54.597 --> 00:01:10.469
I once borrowed a random DVD from the library, and it happened to me how I met you matter and it was the only DVD show that was available in English, so it started watching it and that's how I fell in love with it.
我曾经在图书馆随机借了一张DVD，正好是《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，而且那是唯一一部有英语版本的DVD剧集，所以我开始看它，也就是这样我爱上了它。

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:20.409
I'm alone, oh, be soon in New York City.
我孤身一人，哦，很快就要去纽约了。

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:30.620
When I see you, please permit me to tell you everything in New York City.
当我看到你时，请允许我在纽约告诉你一切。

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.620
Hello, welcome to another episode of How We Made Your Mother.
你好，欢迎收听《老爸老妈的浪漫史》幕后播客的又一期节目。

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.620
I'm your co-host, Josh Radner, and I'm here with my other co-host, Craig Thomas.
我是你的联合主持人乔什·拉德纳，和我在一起的是另一位联合主持人克雷格·托马斯。

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.620
Hey, Craig.
嘿，克雷格。

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.620
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:41.620
Hey.
嘿。

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.620
We're talking about how I met your mother.
我们正在谈论《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.980
That's a show that I starred on from 2005 to 2014.
我在2005年到2014年期间主演了这部剧。

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.019
I played Ted Moseby on that show.
我在剧中饰演泰德·莫斯比。

00:01:49.019 --> 00:01:57.299
Craig co-created the show with Carter Baze and we're sifting back through all of them.
克雷格和卡特·贝斯共同创作了这部剧，现在我们正在重新回看所有这些剧集。

00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:02.299
We're in season two, which episode is this episode 11?
我们正处于第二季，这是第11集吗？

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.530
11 is not exactly half way, exactly half way.
11集不完全是正中间，不过也差不多正中间了。

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.530
We're halfway through the second season.
我们已经看完第二季的一半了。

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:26.689
Just a shout out to that wonderful voice note from our friend who learned English, from watching how I made my mother shout, just a whatever, a hello, a big hug, to all the people around the world that how I made my mother help them learn English.
向我们那位通过看《老爸老妈的浪漫史》来学习英语的朋友发来的精彩语音便签表示感谢，无论如何，向全世界所有因为《老爸老妈的浪漫史》而学会英语的人们问好、给个大大的拥抱。

00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:30.849
It's one of the most delightful things I hear from people.
这是我从人们那里听到最令人高兴的事情之一。

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:33.810
It's one of the coolest things that we hear.
这是我们听到的最酷的事情之一。

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:41.810
If we could get in a time machine and go back, Josh, to 2005 us and be like, people are gonna learn English by watching this show.
如果我们能坐时间机器回到过去，乔什，回到2005年的我们，然后说，人们以后会通过看这部剧来学习英语。

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.689
It's just, it would have been something we weren't thinking in 2005.
这真的是我们在2005年时绝对想不到的。

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:47.969
Yeah, you're not planning that.
对，你根本不会这样去规划。

00:02:47.969 --> 00:02:50.129
You're like, I wonder if this will help teach grammar.
你会想：不知道这能不能帮他们学语法？

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.449
Is the grammar good enough in this one?
这里的语法够不够好？

00:02:53.449 --> 00:02:56.969
ESL teachers around the world will be using this.
全世界的ESL（英语为第二语言）老师都会用这个。

00:02:57.289 --> 00:02:58.889
That's really why we're doing this.
这真的是我们现在在做这件事的原因。

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.969
What a public service.
真是功德无量啊。

00:03:02.169 --> 00:03:03.169
Public service.
功德无量。

00:03:03.169 --> 00:03:06.650
It is the best sort of weird side effect of this show's success.
这是这部剧成功带来的最好的一种奇妙副作用。

00:03:07.009 --> 00:03:09.729
The Dearest really that is such an honor.
天哪，这真的是一种巨大的荣幸。

00:03:09.729 --> 00:03:10.370
It's so cool.
太酷了。

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:15.969
and we hear it all the time, but I don't think, Alec, we've ever had a message directly of someone saying that.
我们经常听到这种说法，但我觉得，埃里克，我们以前从未收到过有人直接发来这样的消息。

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:19.409
So to have that on the record, on the pod is that, that was great.
所以能在播客中正式记录下来，真的很棒。

00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:20.409
Thank you for that message.
谢谢你发来的这条消息。

00:03:20.409 --> 00:03:21.610
Yeah, thank you.
是的，谢谢。

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:28.250
So today we're discussing how Lily stole Christmas, episode 11 of season two.
所以今天我们要讨论的是“莉莉偷走圣诞节”，第二季的第11集。

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.289
This was written, I believe, by Brenda Shea, is that right, Alec?
我想这一集是布伦达·谢伊写的，对吧，埃里克？

00:03:31.289 --> 00:03:32.129
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:03:32.129 --> 00:03:36.979
And when did this air on December 11th of 2006?
这一集是什么时候播出的？2006年12月11日吗？

00:03:36.979 --> 00:03:39.259
Pause to contemplate our mortality.
暂停一下，思考一下我们的终有一死。

00:03:39.259 --> 00:03:41.139
Condo player mortality and we're back.
顺便考虑一下终有一死，我们回来了。

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:48.539
Craig, tell us where this episode's spring from and also just give us a little summary of what this episode's about.
克雷格，告诉我们这一集的灵感是从哪里来的，顺便给我们简单总结一下这一集的内容。

00:03:48.539 --> 00:03:52.300
And if you remember what the genesis for this episode was.
如果你还记得这一集的起源是什么的话。

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.180
Yeah, all of those things.
好的，所有这些。

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.539
Well, first of all, I think it's technically our first Christmas episode.
嗯，首先，我认为这严格来说是我们的第一集圣诞剧集。

00:03:58.539 --> 00:04:02.020
I think this began from, we need to do a Christmas episode.
我想这源于我们觉得：我们需要拍一集圣诞节主题的剧。

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:06.419
We had a Halloween episode in season one, also written by Brenda Scheidt.
我们在第一季有一集万圣节主题的剧，也是布伦达·谢伊写的。

00:04:06.419 --> 00:04:15.819
the slutty pumpkin, we had a Thanksgiving episode, we had a New Year's episode in the limo, we didn't really do a Christmas episode, I think we just didn't have an idea we were excited enough about.
即“荡妇南瓜”，我们还有一集感恩节主题的剧，在加长林肯里还有一集新年主题的剧，但我们一直没拍真正的圣诞节剧，我想我们只是当时没有产生足够让我们兴奋的点子。

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:20.939
I think we technically started the war on Christmas, that's where this political stuff came.
我想严格来说是我们挑起了对圣诞节的战争，政治正确的那一套就是从这里来的。

00:04:20.939 --> 00:04:22.759
They're trying to erase Christmas.
他们正试图抹杀圣诞节。

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:24.819
Yeah, a lot of historians pegging on us.
对，很多历史学家都把这归咎于我们。

00:04:24.819 --> 00:04:25.660
Yeah.
对。

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.980
Briefly contemplating my mortality again, and we're back again.
再次短暂地思考我的终有一死，然后我们又回来了。

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:58.970
No, but it's this episode came from that very universal place of what is the best friend's duty in a breakup is a best friend beholden to say bad things about the person that has dumped said other best friend and this idea we felt like we still had more to explore about Marshall and the police breakup, right?
不，不过这一集的灵感来源于一个非常普遍的问题：在分手时，好朋友的职责到底是什么？好朋友是否有义务说那些甩了另一位好朋友的人的坏话？我们觉得在这个想法上，我们还有更多关于马歇尔和莉莉分手的后续可以挖掘，对吧？

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:05.050
We were broken up for the first however many episodes, right, seven or eight episodes of this season, now this is episode 11.
在前好几集里他们都是处于分手状态的，对吧，这季的前七、八集都是这样，现在这是第11集。

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:08.769
And I remember we thought there's a little more to get out of this, this dramatic situation.
我记得当时我们在想，从这个戏剧性的情境中还能挖掘出更多东西。

00:05:08.769 --> 00:05:15.730
There's a little more, how could something from that breakup in the summer come back around at Christmas time?
还有更多，夏天那场分手中的事情怎么会在圣诞节期间再次卷土重来呢？

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:22.449
And the plot of this episode is there's a recording where Ted says a very bad word about Lily.
这一集的剧情是，有一段录音，里面泰德说了关于莉莉的一个非常难听的词。

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:26.170
And that sets off a series of events that leads to Lily stealing Christmas.
这引发了一系列事件，最终导致莉莉偷走了圣诞节。

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:28.889
I'll allow the Grinch, hence how at least all Christmas.
我就勉为其难当个格林奇吧，因此这集叫《莉莉偷走圣诞节》。

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:40.170
Okay, so my memory is the breakup last until episode seven, but I had forgotten that the reverberations of this like go up until almost there, they walked on the aisle.
好吧，我的记忆是分手持续到了第七集，但我忘了这场分手的余波一直延续到他们几乎走进婚礼殿堂。

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:57.730
Like, it's a much longer kind of tremor or like aftershock than I had remembered, but I'm glad to see that because it does feel like, one thing I admire about you and Carter and the writers, like you were always, I think, pausing to go, what is the real thing happening?
就像是，这比我记忆中持续时间更长、波及范围更大的余震，但我很高兴看到这一点，因为这确实让人觉得——我非常佩服你、卡特还有编剧们的一点是——你们总是会停下来思考：真正发生的事情是什么？

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:09.529
Like what would be the real, the most real thing that we could then spin into comedy gold, but like let's, it just feels like you're really giving this like it's proper due and it's breath.
就像什么是真正的事情，最真实的事情，然后我们可以把它转化成喜剧的黄金素材，但这就好像你们真的给予了它应有的重视和呼吸空间。

00:06:09.529 --> 00:06:10.370
Yeah.
对。

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.329
Yeah, we really wanted to let things echo out.
是的，我们真的很想让事情产生回响。

00:06:12.329 --> 00:06:19.850
We didn't like that sitcom convention where every week's a new week and the Etchus Sketch has been shaken up and it's just a tabular rasa.
我们不喜欢那种老套的情景喜剧惯例：每周都是新的一周，画板已经被擦得干干净净，一切都归零。

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:22.089
It's like, no, people hold on to things.
情况是，不，人们会把事情记在心里。

00:06:22.089 --> 00:06:23.410
Shit comes back around.
破事儿总是会卷土重来的。

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:24.410
There's nothing is neat.
没有什么事情是干干净净的。

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.730
There's no, things aren't in need containers.
没有，事情都不是装在整洁的容器里的。

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.370
And that's what this episode comes from.
这就是这一集的灵感来源。

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:38.569
It's very funny how much old technology, namely an answering machine, the entire plot of this episode is hinged on an answering machine message.
很有趣的是，这么多的老旧技术——也就是答录机——这一集的整个剧情竟然全靠一条答录机留言支撑。

00:06:38.569 --> 00:06:40.649
That can be played out loud and heard by Andy.
它可以被大声播放出来，并被安迪听到。

00:06:40.649 --> 00:06:42.410
You can't recreate this episode today.
你今天无法再重现这样一集了。

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:43.930
Like this is not an episode.
就像这不是现在会有的剧情。

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:45.050
Why do they have that?
为什么他们会有那个？

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.329
That's still, I technically still have an answering machine.
我其实，严格来说我家里现在还留着一台答录机。

00:06:48.329 --> 00:06:51.290
I have a landslide with an answering machine and people make fun of me.
我有一部带答录机的座机，人们还因此取笑我。

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:54.587
Do you know what's so funny when everyone went on to phones.
你知道最搞笑的是什么吗，当所有人都换了手机的时候。

00:06:54.587 --> 00:07:06.895
I remember screenwriters saying, man, the answering machine, the public, you know, the answering machine where you press it and the voice goes into the room was such a useful platform, like a useful device for screenwriters.
我记得编剧们曾说过，天哪，答录机，公众都知道的，你可以按一下然后声音就会在房间里放出来的那个答录机，对编剧来说曾是一个多么好用的平台、多么好用的设备啊。

00:07:06.895 --> 00:07:07.829
And it went away.
然后它就消失了。

00:07:07.829 --> 00:07:08.329
Yeah.
对。

00:07:08.329 --> 00:07:08.829
Yeah.
对。

00:07:08.829 --> 00:07:12.093
Remember Ted breaking up with, uh, uh, in season one?
还记得在第一季里泰德和……呃……分手的那个情节吗？

00:07:12.093 --> 00:07:12.647
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

00:07:12.647 --> 00:07:14.125
Uh, return to the shirt.
呃，把衬衫退回去。

00:07:14.125 --> 00:07:17.389
He's breaking up with his, it's, forgive me, Natalie.
他在和他的——原谅我，娜塔莉。

00:07:17.389 --> 00:07:18.682
I'm breaking out her.
我在和她分手。

00:07:18.682 --> 00:07:19.182
Natalie.
娜塔莉。

00:07:19.182 --> 00:07:19.682
Yeah.
对。

00:07:19.682 --> 00:07:20.182
Yeah.
对。

00:07:20.182 --> 00:07:23.445
That's another big answering machine, uh, plot point.
那也是另一个重要的答录机……呃，剧情关键点。

00:07:23.445 --> 00:07:36.253
I mean, just a little flashback with the idea of how much worse it was made by hearing heads voice bringing up that are on the answering machine and a whole surprise party of all their friends are hearing it.
我是说，只需一个小小的情节闪回，一想到听到老朋友的声音在答录机里说那些话，而且他们的一群朋友都在听，情况变得有多糟糕。

00:07:36.253 --> 00:07:40.625
We have a few of those were really hinging ideas on answering machines.
我们有几个情节真的是把点子押在答录机上的。

00:07:40.625 --> 00:07:43.704
It doesn't make us look super young at this point.
从现在来看，这显得我们可真不怎么年轻。

00:07:43.704 --> 00:07:48.322
I was actually just weirdly discussing this with some friends this weekend.
其实这个周末我还真跟一些朋友怪异地讨论过这件事。

00:07:48.322 --> 00:07:55.465
That kind of space between when your friend breaks up with to someone, how long do you wait to talk shit about them?
就是当你朋友和某人分手后，到那种程度，你要等多久才能说前任的坏话？

00:07:54.689 --> 00:07:58.850
Is it your duty to immediately say they were whore?
你的责任是不是立刻说他们是个贱人？

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.930
You actually don't want to because there's a window when they still might get back together.
你其实又不想这么做，因为有一个窗口期，他们可能还会复合。

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:02.769
They might get back together.
他们可能会复合。

00:08:02.769 --> 00:08:05.290
You can't talk too much shit about them, right?
你不能把对方贬得太惨，对吧？

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:06.769
Like you have to be like.
就像你必须得表现得……

00:08:06.769 --> 00:08:09.730
That would have been a great observation to put in this episode, what you just said.
你刚才说的这个观察要是能放进这一集里，那就太棒了。

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:13.050
I have overcome with regret that we don't have that observation in the episode.
我感到十分懊悔，我们没能把这个观察放进这集里。

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:20.810
But what I loved, one of my favorite parts of this episode was that it was revealed, it was Ted's anger at Lily that was fueling it.
但我最喜欢的、这一集里我最喜欢的桥段之一，就是剧情揭晓，是泰德对莉莉的愤怒在推波助澜。

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.529
He wasn't just being a partisan on behalf of his friend.
他不只是在替朋友当坚定的拥护者。

00:08:23.529 --> 00:08:25.250
He also felt like he got left.
他也觉得自己被抛下了。

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:26.329
You broke up with me.
你跟我绝交了。

00:08:26.329 --> 00:08:27.769
You broke up with me as a friend.
你作为朋友跟我绝交了。

00:08:27.769 --> 00:08:28.610
You didn't call me.
你甚至没给我打电话。

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:34.649
You know that one episode where he literally calls him his Ted, her best friend, you know?
你知道那一集吗，他字面意义上称呼他是泰德——她的最好朋友，你知道吗？

00:08:34.649 --> 00:08:46.330
There's like a wounding and a rupture that Ted also felt and Ted was left with this baby, with this crying baby in the form of, you know, broken up Marshall.
泰德也感受到了某种创伤和决裂，而且泰德还留下来面对这个“婴儿”，面对痛苦分手的马歇尔这个哭泣的婴儿。

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:47.169
So.
所以。

00:08:47.169 --> 00:08:48.409
I think that's what unlocks the episode.
我觉得这就是解开这一集密码的关键。

00:08:48.409 --> 00:08:54.049
That piece late in the episode where Ted, for the first time in the episode, says what it's really about for him.
剧集后半部分的那个片段，泰德在这集中第一次说出了对他来说真正意味着什么。

00:08:54.049 --> 00:08:58.289
